---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro'
import { alts } from '../../data/compare'

const DEMO = 'https://demo.cartapel.com'
const DOCS = 'https://docs.cartapel.com'
const GH = 'https://github.com/De-Rus/cartapel'
---

<Base
  title="cartapel vs Django admin, Retool, Metabase & friends"
  description="Honest comparison of database admin panels: cartapel vs Django admin, Retool, Metabase, NocoDB, Baserow, Directus and pgAdmin — who each tool is for, and where cartapel is not the right choice."
  path="/compare"
  ogTitle="cartapel vs the alternatives"
>
  <script
    slot="head"
    type="application/ld+json"
    set:html={JSON.stringify({
      '@context': 'https://schema.org',
      '@type': 'CollectionPage',
      name: 'cartapel vs the alternatives',
      description:
        'Honest comparison of cartapel with Django admin, Retool, Metabase, NocoDB, Baserow, Directus and pgAdmin.',
      url: 'https://cartapel.com/compare/',
      isPartOf: { '@type': 'WebSite', name: 'cartapel', url: 'https://cartapel.com/' },
      hasPart: alts.map((a) => ({
        '@type': 'WebPage',
        name: a.h1,
        url: `https://cartapel.com/compare/${a.slug}/`,
      })),
    })}
  />

  <div class="page-head">
    <h1>cartapel vs the alternatives</h1>
    <p class="sub">
      There are many good ways to put an admin UI in front of a database. These pages are the
      comparison a fair-minded engineer would trust: what cartapel does well, what each
      alternative does <em>better</em>, and when you should pick them instead. Start with the
      matrix, then open the one you are actually weighing.
    </p>
    <nav class="toc" aria-label="On this page">
      <a href="#glance">At a glance</a>
      {alts.map((a) => (
        <a href={`/compare/${a.slug}/`}>{a.name}</a>
      ))}
      <a href="#not">When not cartapel</a>
    </nav>
  </div>

  <section id="glance">
    <h2>At a glance</h2>
    <div class="glance">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th></th>
            <th>cartapel</th>
            {alts.map((a) => <th><a href={`/compare/${a.slug}/`}>{a.column}</a></th>)}
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Databases</td>
            <td class="yes">Postgres, MySQL, MariaDB (+ ClickHouse RO)</td>
            <td class="mid">via Django models</td>
            <td class="yes">many sources</td>
            <td class="yes">many sources</td>
            <td class="yes">several SQL DBs</td>
            <td class="mid">Postgres/MySQL + own metadata</td>
            <td class="mid">prefers its own tables</td>
            <td class="no">Postgres only</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Single binary</td>
            <td class="yes">one Rust binary</td>
            <td class="no">part of a Python app</td>
            <td class="no">multi-container / SaaS</td>
            <td class="no">JVM app</td>
            <td class="no">Node app</td>
            <td class="no">Node services</td>
            <td class="no">Python + Node services</td>
            <td class="no">Python app</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Existing DB, no schema ownership</td>
            <td class="yes">introspects live schema</td>
            <td class="mid">ORM models to sync</td>
            <td class="yes">queries any source</td>
            <td class="yes">read-oriented</td>
            <td class="yes">introspects both ways</td>
            <td class="mid">connects, adds metadata</td>
            <td class="no">import-oriented</td>
            <td class="yes">raw access</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Config as code / PR review</td>
            <td class="yes">HCL in git — visual editor or by hand</td>
            <td class="yes">Python in git</td>
            <td class="mid">GUI-first; git sync paid</td>
            <td class="mid">GUI-first; serialize paid</td>
            <td class="mid">config in DB; snapshots</td>
            <td class="no">GUI-configured</td>
            <td class="no">GUI-configured</td>
            <td class="no">n/a</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Roles + audit</td>
            <td class="yes">inheritance, audit + revert</td>
            <td class="mid">roles solid; audit via packages</td>
            <td class="mid">full audit on higher tiers</td>
            <td class="mid">sandboxing is paid</td>
            <td class="yes">granular roles, revisions</td>
            <td class="mid">base/table roles</td>
            <td class="mid">history varies by tier</td>
            <td class="no">Postgres roles only</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Dashboards</td>
            <td class="yes">SQL tiles, charts, vars</td>
            <td class="no">not built in</td>
            <td class="yes">build anything</td>
            <td class="yes">best-in-class BI</td>
            <td class="mid">insights panels</td>
            <td class="mid">basic views/charts</td>
            <td class="mid">basic views/charts</td>
            <td class="no">DBA charts only</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>License + price</td>
            <td class="yes">MIT, free</td>
            <td class="yes">BSD, free</td>
            <td class="no">proprietary, per-user</td>
            <td class="mid">AGPL core; paid Pro</td>
            <td class="mid">BSL 1.1</td>
            <td class="mid">AGPL / open-core</td>
            <td class="mid">MIT core / paid tiers</td>
            <td class="yes">Postgres license, free</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="glance-note">Yellow cells are nuanced — open the page for that tool before deciding.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>One page per alternative</h2>
    <p class="sub" style="margin: 0 0 20px; max-width: 680px; font-size: 16px;">
      Each is written to be useful even if you end up choosing the other tool.
    </p>
    <div class="grid other-grid">
      {alts.map((a) => (
        <a class="card" href={`/compare/${a.slug}/`}>
          <h3>cartapel vs {a.name}</h3>
          <p>{a.card}</p>
        </a>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="not">
    <h2>Where cartapel is not the right choice</h2>
    <ul class="not-list">
      <li><strong>Already on Django with real models</strong> — use <a href="/compare/django-admin/">Django admin</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>Apps over many data sources, not a panel over your databases</strong> — <a href="/compare/retool/">Retool</a> (or Appsmith / Budibase).</li>
      <li><strong>Analytics and self-serve exploration</strong> — <a href="/compare/metabase/">Metabase</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>Non-technical users owning data in a spreadsheet UI</strong> — <a href="/compare/nocodb/">NocoDB</a> or <a href="/compare/baserow/">Baserow</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>Generated API, automations and a CMS with the admin</strong> — <a href="/compare/directus/">Directus</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>Administering the database server itself</strong> — <a href="/compare/pgadmin/">pgAdmin</a>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p style="margin-top: 28px; max-width: 680px; font-size: 15.5px;">
      What cartapel is for: a team with an existing production database that wants a safe,
      audited, role-scoped operations panel — one binary, config reviewable in a pull request.
    </p>
    <div class="cta" style="justify-content: flex-start; margin-top: 22px;">
      <a class="btn primary" href={DEMO}>Try the live demo</a>
      <a class="btn" href={`${DOCS}/getting-started`}>Get started</a>
      <a class="btn ghost" href={GH}>GitHub</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
